{
  "id": "proj-pallet-wall-planter",
  "slug": "pallet-wall-planter",
  "status": "published",
  "title": "Pallet Wall Planter",
  "summary": "A vertical herb garden from one heat-treated pallet. Landscape fabric pockets hold the soil.",
  "difficulty": "beginner",
  "estimatedHours": 2,
  "imageUrl": null,
  "currency": "USD",
  "steps": [
    "Check the pallet for the HT (heat-treated) stamp and reject any marked MB (methyl bromide) or stained with oil; scrub it with a stiff brush and let it dry.",
    "Sand the face boards with 80 then 120 grit until no splinters catch a rag.",
    "Cut the cedar pickets to the inside width of the pallet and screw one across the bottom of each opening you want to plant, spanning from the front slat to the back boards, with 2 1/2 in deck screws. Each opening becomes a trough with a floor.",
    "Staple a double layer of landscape fabric to the back, sides and floor of every trough, pleating the corners so soil cannot escape; trim the excess with the utility knife.",
    "Lay the pallet flat, fill each trough with potting mix to 1 in below the slat, plant or sow the herbs and water until it drains from the lowest row.",
    "Leave it flat for one to two weeks so the roots bind the soil, then stand it up and fasten the top stringer to a wall stud or fence post with two 3/8 in lag screws."
  ],
